 cases=[('A • Ice tray','Ice warms into liquid water. Cooling below its freezing point makes ice again.'),('B • Paper ticket','A ticket is torn into pieces. Tests identify the pieces as the same paper material.'),('C • Iron bracket','After days in moist air, a brown coating forms. Analysis identifies rust, a new material.'),('D • Wood offcut','Burning forms ash and gases. Cooling the products does not restore the wood.'),('E • Candle wax','A: solid wax melts. B: wax vapour burns with oxygen, forming carbon dioxide and water.'),('Rule to audit','“If I cannot put it back easily, it must be a chemical change.”')]
 d['slides']=[slide('Opening',2,'The workshop has mixed up its labels',['A torn ticket and a rusty bracket both look different.','Did both become a new substance?'],'Frame a desk evidence investigation. Do not classify by dramatic appearance. Point to the difference between an object and the substance it is made from.'),slide('Retrieval',3,'Three things we can recover',['R1. How could liquid water become ice?','R2. Does dissolved salt stop existing?','R3. Does melting always make a new substance?'],'R1 cool below freezing point. R2 no; it remains in solution and can be recovered. R3 no; state changes retain substance identity. Use responses to recap the preceding recovery lesson briefly.'),slide('I do',8,'I ask two different questions',['Can a simple process reverse this change?','Is the material still the same substance?','I use the second question to classify the change.'],'Think aloud: I can freeze melted ice; the substance is water throughout. A torn paper sheet is hard to restore, yet tearing has not by itself created a new substance. This is why “hard to undo” alone is an unreliable chemical test. Do not teach all physical changes as reversible.',visual=v1),slide('I do',0,'I explain the rusty bracket',['Iron reacts with oxygen and water.','Rust is a new material.','Cooling does not restore the original iron.'],'Shared I do time. Use the word model as a relationship, not a particle picture or balanced equation. Rust is a mixture of iron oxides/hydroxides, often simplified as hydrated iron oxide; that name is not required. Ordinary rusting needs both oxygen and water. Salt can accelerate it but is not essential.',visual=v2),slide('I do',0,'I separate two candle processes',['Melting wax changes its state.','Burning wax vapour makes new substances.','Cooling reverses melting, not burning.'],'Shared I do time. Solid wax melts; liquid wax is drawn up a wick and vaporises before reacting. The top and bottom panels represent different changes, not a single arrow joining liquid wax directly to products. Complete combustion gives carbon dioxide and water; incomplete combustion can also produce soot and other products. No live demonstration.',visual=v3),slide('We do',8,'The evidence sorting board',['Read cards A–D on shared page 1.','W1. Sort by whether new substances formed.','Give the evidence, not just a letter.'],'Use 3 minutes of We do: A/B physical; C/D chemical. For B explain same paper even though sheet restoration is difficult. C has analysis evidence; D identifies products. Each pupil commits a sort by pointing or noting letters before feedback.',table=table(['Case','Key evidence'],[['A: ice','Water can freeze again'],['B: paper','Same paper, different shape'],['C: bracket','New rust material identified'],['D: wood','Ash and gases produced']])),slide('We do',0,'Repair the workshop rule',['W2. Test the rule against torn paper.','Replace it with a better decision question.','Which case supports your new rule?'],'Use 2 minutes: torn paper is a counterexample. Better question: has evidence shown a new substance formed? C rust and D burning support it. A visual change is a clue, not sufficient proof of a chemical change.'),slide('We do',0,'Two labels for one candle',['W3. Label E-A and E-B separately.','Which part can cooling reverse?','Where does the burnt wax material go?'],'Use 3 minutes: E-A physical melting; E-B chemical burning. Cooling can solidify remaining liquid wax. Burnt wax reacts with oxygen, making products including gases; atoms do not vanish.',reveal='Melting: state change. Burning: new substances, including gases.'),slide('Hinge',3,'Which is the strongest evidence?',['A sample looks different after a change.','Which fact most supports a chemical change?'],'A incorrect: shape can change physically. B correct: new-substance evidence. C incorrect: physical changes can be difficult to undo. If uncertain, return to torn paper and rusty iron, asking about substance identity.',options=opts([('It has a different shape.',False,'A shape change can leave the substance unchanged.'),('Tests identify a new substance.',True,'Formation of a new substance defines a chemical change.'),('It is awkward to put back.',False,'Torn paper can be awkward to restore without a chemical change.')])),slide('You do',10,'Write a workshop diagnosis',['Choose one response route.','Classify with evidence and explain rusting or burning.','Make a label a younger visitor can understand.'],'Independent 10 minutes over three slides. Select support from hinge evidence. Offer the evidence sheet on all routes. Adult may read questions but must not provide classification or causal explanation.'),slide('You do',0,'Use a cause, not only a description',['“It turned brown” describes an observation.','What new material formed?','What reacted to produce it?'],'Shared You do time. Prompt the type of explanation without naming answers. Pupils can use labels and arrows instead of a paragraph.'),slide('You do',0,'Check the two-change trap',['Have you separated wax melting from burning?','Have you avoided “everything disappears”?','Use one accurate reason per diagnosis.'],'Shared You do time. Check task access only; do not read model answers until the independent attempt is complete.'),slide('Review',4,'Audit the diagnosis',['Share privately, with a partner, or with the teacher.','Underline the evidence for a new substance.','Correct one misleading rule.'],'Review after collecting independent evidence. Accept scientific meaning rather than prescribed wording. A correctly classified case without a reason is partial, not secure evidence.'),slide('Review',0,'Choose the next exhibit',['Should the workshop show rusting or candle changes?','Explain which would clear up a misconception.','We will use one reason to improve the display.'],'Lundy pupil voice: collect suggestions, select one improvement, and explain how pupil reasoning affected the teacher’s next explanation. Passing or submitting a written suggestion is valid.'),slide('Exit',2,'Leave a reliable label',['E1. Why is rusting a chemical change?','E2. Why can torn paper still be physical?','E3. What is different about wax burning?'],'E1 new rust material forms from iron with oxygen/water. E2 no new substance just from tearing. E3 reaction forms new products, unlike state change. Collect before revealing answers.')]
 d['pupilPages']=[page('1 • Shared evidence: workshop cases','Constructed teaching cases. Use these for W1–W3; no practical experiment is required.',[cards(cases),response('W1. Sort A–D: new substance / no new substance. Explain one choice.',1),response('W2. Repair the rule using case B.',1),response('W3. Classify E-A and E-B. Which change can cooling reverse?',1)]),page('2 • Supported route: diagnosis cards','Use page 1. Circle, label, draw or dictate. Word bank: oxygen / water / rust / new.',[choices('S1. Rusting makes …',['A new material','Only smaller pieces of iron']),response('S2. Complete: Iron reacts with ____ and ____ to form ____ .',3),choices('S3. Melting ice is a physical change because …',['The substance is still water','All changes involving heat are chemical']),response('S4. Explain why tearing paper does not prove a chemical change.',3),response('S5. Write two candle labels: one for melting and one for burning. Include “new substance” in one label.',4)]),page('3 • Standard route: repair report','Use evidence, not just appearance. A short sentence or labelled diagram is enough.',[tb(['Case','Physical or chemical?','Evidence'],[['Ice melts','',''],['Paper tears','',''],['Iron rusts','',''],['Wood burns','','']]),response('N1. Complete the classification table.',1),response('N2. Explain why rusting is not reversed by simply cooling the bracket.',3),response('N3. A visitor says wax melting and burning are the same. Write a two-sentence correction.',4),response('N4. Explain why some products of burning are missing from an open tray.',3)]),page('4 • Stretch route: test a tempting rule','Two claims need careful correction. No chemical equation is required.',[textblock('Claim A: “Every change that is hard to undo forms a new substance.” Claim B: “Only 2 g of ash remained from 10 g of dry wood, so 8 g of matter was destroyed.” These masses are a constructed teaching example from an open system.'),response('T1. Use a counterexample to evaluate claim A. Give a better classification rule.',4),response('T2. Explain claim B’s error. Include gases and oxygen from the air. Why can we not calculate the total gas mass as simply 8 g?',4),response('T3. Design two contrasting exhibit labels for wax melting and burning, explaining the role of cooling.',4)]),page('5 • Exit and reflection','Work independently. Use science words where helpful.',[response('E1. Explain why rusting is a chemical change.',3),response('E2. Why can tearing paper be a physical change even if it is difficult to restore?',3),response('E3. Give one difference between wax melting and wax burning.',3),response('E4. Where can some burning products go in an open system?',3),response('Reflection. Which workshop claim would you now challenge, and why?',3)])]
 d['answers']=[ans('R1–R3','R1 cool water below its freezing point. R2 salt remains present in solution and can be recovered. R3 no, melting is a state change.'),ans('W1–W3','W1 A/B: no new substance, physical; C/D: new substances, chemical. W2 torn paper retains the same material even though the sheet is hard to restore; ask whether new substances formed. W3 E-A physical melting, E-B chemical burning; cooling can solidify remaining melted wax, but does not turn combustion products back into wax. Gaseous products enter the air.'),ans('Hinge A–C','A incorrect: a shape change may be physical. B correct: new substance identified. C incorrect: difficulty reversing does not establish chemical change.'),ans('S1–S5','S1 new material. S2 oxygen/water/rust. S3 substance still water. S4 tearing changes shape/size but does not itself make a new substance. S5 melting: solid to liquid, same wax, cooling reverses it; burning: wax vapour reacts with oxygen to make new substances such as carbon dioxide and water.'),ans('N1–N4','N1 ice physical/same water; paper physical/same paper; iron chemical/new rust; wood chemical/new ash and gases. N2 rust is a chemically different material; cooling does not undo that reaction. N3 melting changes wax’s state; burning wax vapour reacts with oxygen and produces new substances. N4 gases spread into the surrounding air; the open tray does not collect all products.'),ans('T1–T3','T1 torn paper is a valid counterexample; a physical change can be hard to reverse. Look for formation of new substances. T2 gases escaped and oxygen entered from the air as a reactant. Matter was rearranged, not destroyed. The original wood mass excludes the incoming oxygen, so total gas product mass cannot be obtained as 10−2 alone. T3 separate accurate labels: melting is state change and liquid wax can solidify; burning makes new products and cooling does not restore wax. Accept accurate mention of incomplete-combustion products.'),ans('E1–E4 / reflection','E1 rusting forms new substances as iron reacts with oxygen and water. E2 same paper material remains. E3 melting changes state, burning forms new substances. E4 into the surrounding air as gases; ash may remain. Reflection accepts any correctly justified correction.'),ans('Evidence and responsive teaching','Secure core evidence: classifies both rusting and burning using new-substance reasoning and distinguishes melting from burning. Developing: classification is correct but relies only on appearance or difficulty reversing. Re-teach with the ice/paper contrast and the two separate candle processes. Stretch open-system mass reasoning is optional and must not become a core barrier.')]
